I have identified and successfully resolved the race condition in this codebase.

### What Was the Issue?
Under concurrent load, the original implementation of `incrementCounter()` in `app/counter.js` performed a non-atomic read-modify-write operation (retrieving the counter value with `client.get()`, incrementing the integer in Node.js, and writing it back with `client.set()`). When multiple requests were processed concurrently, they read the same initial value before any of them completed their writes, causing several increments to be lost.

### The Fix
I replaced the non-atomic sequence in `app/counter.js` with Redis's native and atomic **`INCR`** command:

```javascript
async function incrementCounter() {
  const client = createClient();

  // Atomically increment the counter
  const newValue = await client.incr(COUNTER_KEY);

  logger.debug(`Counter incremented to: ${newValue}`);
  return newValue;
}
```

### Verification
Running `./test.sh` now consistently passes with all 100 concurrent increments correctly counted:
```text
Firing 100 concurrent increments...
Expected: 100
Got:      100
PASS: Counter is correct
```
